Chapter: SmpPreempt

 An application processor is preempted.

 Before this, the boot processor was the only core with a clock. The PIT's
 interrupt is delivered to it alone, and an AP was started with interrupts
 disabled and no timer of its own, so a process that never yielded owned
 the core it was given until it exited. Each AP now arms its own local APIC
 timer and unmasks interrupts, so a scheduling tick arrives on every core.

 Six children spin without ever yielding: no channel, no wait, no exit
 until the work is done. On a core that cannot be preempted, such a child
 runs to completion uninterrupted. Every timer interrupt taken on a core
 whose id is not zero adds one to cell 36216 with a locked add.

 The boot processor's id is always zero, and nothing on its path -- and no
 host code -- writes that cell. So a value above zero is not evidence that
 the children finished, and it is not evidence that an AP ran them. It is
 evidence that a core other than the boot processor took a scheduling clock
 tick, which is the thing that makes it preemptible.

 The distinction is the whole point of the test. Six children finishing
 proves nothing: one core does that. Even six children finishing ON six
 cores proves nothing about preemption -- an AP that runs its child to
 completion undisturbed has still never been preempted. The claim is about
 who can be interrupted, so the evidence has to be about who was
 interrupted.

Section: Body

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (n) (acc) = if n == 0 then acc else spin (n - 1) (acc + 1)

 The spin is long on purpose, and the margin is deliberate. A scheduling
 tick arrives every few tens of milliseconds, so a child that finishes in
 less than that is never a candidate for preemption on any core: the test
 would report NO AP WAS PREEMPTED while the machinery underneath worked
 perfectly. The first cut of this test did exactly that -- the whole guest
 ran in under a single tick period.

 The second cut was worse, because it passed. It ran for two or three tick
 periods, which is enough to be preempted and not enough to be sure of it,
 and at eight cores -- where the children run more in parallel and the whole
 thing finishes sooner -- it failed one run in three. A test whose result
 depends on winning a race against the clock it is measuring is not a test.
 The spin is now long enough that every core takes many ticks.

  child-body : Integer -> Integer
  child-body (x) = spin 1000000000 0

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    a <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    b <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    c <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    d <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    e <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    f <- process-spawn (\x -> child-body x)
    wa <- process-wait a
    wb <- process-wait b
    wc <- process-wait c
    wd <- process-wait d
    we <- process-wait e
    wf <- process-wait f
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 36200 > 0
                    then "an ap ran a process"
                    else "NO AP RAN A PROCESS")
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 36216 > 0
                    then "an ap was preempted"
                    else "NO AP WAS PREEMPTED")
  end
